From 9d774aeee16ba2cab0c3f14968304406736f9c22 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>
Date: Thu, 3 Sep 2026 19:21:12 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] publish: one tag scheme for every port, typescript included

typescript released under a BARE `v<version>` while every other port -- and
every sibling repository -- uses `<lang>/v<version>`. The bare tags are not
wrong, but being the one exception cost more than it saved.

The tag is this repository's release marker, and the tooling reads one by
matching `^([a-z+]+)/v`. A bare tag does not match, so
tools/release_status.py silently dropped every typescript release and
reported the port at `typescript/v0.2.1` -- a stale tag from an old Makefile
publish -- while 0.3.4 was live on npm. tools/bump.py needed a special case
in its table for the same reason. Worse, the repository cut the same release
under two different tags depending on the path taken: publish.yml wrote
`v<version>`, typescript/Makefile wrote `typescript/v<version>`, and the
`repo-tag` npm script wrote the bare one again.

All three write `typescript/v<version>` now, and publish.yml's push trigger
takes `typescript/v*` in place of `v*`. bump.py's table loses its exception.
AGENTS.md, the root Makefile and design/PUBLISH_AUTOMATION.md lose the
paragraphs that existed to explain the inconsistency.

THE FOUR FILES MUST LAND TOGETHER. bump.py composes the tag that
publish.yml's trigger listens for, so shipping the tooling half alone would
cut `typescript/v0.3.5` at a workflow still waiting on `v*` -- a tag push
that silently publishes nothing, which is worse than the inconsistency it
replaces.

The 16 bare tags stay. They are what those releases went out under, and
rewriting a published release marker to tidy a scheme would be worse than
the inconsistency itself. The namespaces sit side by side: everything up to
`v0.3.4` bare, everything after it under `typescript/v`. Until the first
release is cut under the new scheme, bump.py reports 0.3.4 as untagged --
accurate, since no `typescript/v0.3.4` exists, and self-correcting the
moment one is cut. typescript is deliberately left at 0.3.4 here: the
release that cuts that first tag belongs with a real change, not with this.

Also corrects the workflow header's @voxgig/struct-js bootstrap
instructions, which said a `target: javascript` dispatch would fail the OIDC
exchange. Its trusted publisher is registered now, and leaving that text
would send the next release down a token path for no reason.

